[Applause]  hello texas  [Applause]  they came out in force to protest the  country's newest and most restrictive  abortion law abortions after a fetal  heartbeat is detectable at about six  weeks of gestation are now illegal in  texas that's before most women even know  they're pregnant i don't think that you  know  old  men should like politicians should be  making the decision like the decision of  what i can and can't do with my body  and  i think everyone like every woman should  have the right to decide when they want  to have kids and how they want to have  kids and how many  the texas law took effect in september  the bill that i'm about to sign that  ensures that the life of every unborn  child who has a heartbeat will be saved  from the ravages of abortion  the law offers a financial reward to  private citizens who successfully sue  anyone who performs or enables an  abortion in texas doctors nurses even  uber drivers are at risk of civil  penalties  attorney elizabeth meyers says she too  could be sued for representing abortion  clinics by the letter of the law i am in  violation of it  and the law is unconstitutional so it's  not really a law  what's your message to those who might  want to see you  bring it  a few thousand people have gathered at  the texas state capitol to protest the  ban they call it  unconstitutional and resulting in women  being forced to continue with unwanted  pregnancies or having to travel to other  states for abortions if they can afford  it  but eventually abortions may be banned  in all states that's the fear of  marchers who also rallied saturday in  washington and other major u.s cities  they worry the country's supreme court  is poised to overturn roe versus wade  the landmark case that gave american  women the right to an abortion in 1973.  julia kirkland says she had an abortion  years ago when doctors said her fetus  had a fatal medical condition  that same procedure would be illegal in  texas today there's a very real risk  that my uterus would have ruptured if i  weren't allowed to have the abortion  and although my life might have been  saved  i could never have had children after  that she went on to become a mother of  three by choice  she says she hopes other women will be  able to decide for themselves as well  heidi jo castro al jazeera austin texas  well wanna brooks is a civil rights  attorney she says large demonstrations  like these could change the course of  abortion laws  i do think it'll make a difference  as far as the dialogue and as far as  continuing to to keep attention on the  subject matter of course and for all of  those who are passionate about it but  will it make a difference as to what uh  the supreme court may ultimately do or  the judge that is currently uh dealing  with uh the the lawsuit brought by the  justice department that's difficult to  say um you know ideally or typically it  shouldn't make a difference um as far as  in the court of public opinion because  of course judges are supposed to be  neutral um however as we saw from the  supreme court um last month when they  when they failed to or rather they chose  not to um not to hear uh you know  whether or not that they should  interfere with the texas law and it was  a five to four vote and what what we did  see though is that they did leave a door  open to the possibility that they may in  fact entertain um hearing from  the justice department and making a  ruling on this in the future again on  face value theoretically it shouldn't  make a difference to judicial officers  who are supposed to be neutral but do i  think the court of public opinion has uh  carries weight of course it does of  course it does and people have been  extremely vocal and are very passionate  about this issue

